Vitre session for Fedora and Arch
=================================

What this package adds
  * "Vitre" in the session menu on the login screen (/usr/share/wayland-sessions/vitre.desktop). Your normal GNOME session stays
    installed and selectable.
  * /usr/share/gnome-shell/modes/vitre.json: the GNOME Shell session mode. It lists the extensions to load (the distribution's tray
    icon and tiling extensions, then the Vitre ones; a uuid that is not installed is skipped) and inherits everything else from GNOME's
    "user" mode.
  * The shell theme, in one layer: GNOME's own stylesheet, with Vitre-Glass (/usr/share/themes/Vitre-Glass/gnome-shell) on top of it
    through the user-theme extension, whose name key is set in the Vitre defaults. This is what a per-user install of Vitre does on
    Fedora and Arch. (The .debs for Ubuntu put a second layer underneath, a Vitre shell theme resource built on Yaru's stylesheets.)
  * /etc/dconf/profile/vitre, /etc/dconf/db/vitre.d/00-vitre and 10-gnome-parity: system defaults (theme, icons, cursor, wallpaper, fonts,
    keybindings and the settings of the extensions; 10-gnome-parity repeats the behaviour Vitre was tuned with on Ubuntu: start in the
    desktop instead of the Overview, hot corners off, tap to click, Alt+Tab switching windows, and a few more). Only the Vitre session
    reads them: /usr/bin/vitre-session sets DCONF_PROFILE=vitre, your normal GNOME session does not.
  * A per-user step, vitre-user-setup.service, started by the Vitre session only. It links the Vitre GTK stylesheets
    (/usr/share/vitre/gtk-3.0 and gtk-4.0) into ~/.config/gtk-3.0 and ~/.config/gtk-4.0 once, never over a file that is already there.
    GTK 4 and libadwaita read their stylesheet only from the user's config folder, which is why this step exists.

The order of the dconf profile
  user-db:user      your own settings: a value you set yourself always wins (dconf reset <key> gives the default back)
  system-db:local   machine-wide settings of this computer's administrator
  system-db:site    machine-wide settings of a site or organisation
  system-db:vitre   the Vitre defaults
  First match wins. dconf warns in every program when a database named in the profile does not exist, so the package creates
  /etc/dconf/db/local.d and site.d (empty, with a README.vitre note) when neither the folder nor the database exists, and runs
  "dconf update". An existing database is never touched; on removal the folders go again only while the note is the only file in them.

What reaches your normal GNOME session
  * The GTK links above: GTK 3 and GTK 4 applications read ~/.config/gtk-3.0 and gtk-4.0 in every session, so after the first Vitre
    login they show the Vitre style in your normal session too. /usr/lib/vitre/vitre-user-setup --remove takes the links out again.
  * Settings you change while in the Vitre session are saved in your own account, which both sessions read. The Vitre defaults
    themselves never reach the other session.

How to go back
  Pick your normal GNOME session on the login screen. To remove Vitre completely, remove vitre-desktop, vitre-session and the
  other vitre-* packages (and vitre-system if you installed it, which puts the previous boot splash back). Run
  /usr/lib/vitre/vitre-user-setup --remove first if you want the GTK links gone as well.

Differences from the Ubuntu packages
  * No Ubuntu session extensions (ubuntu-dock is not wanted anyway: the Vitre Dock is the only dock); the tray icon extension
    (appindicatorsupport) and tiling-assistant are listed if you have them.
  * 10-gnome-parity is 10-ubuntu-parity without its Ubuntu-only parts (the favourite applications, which name Ubuntu apps, and the
    settings of Ubuntu's desktop icons extension). It gives the Vitre session the values Ubuntu's per-desktop overrides give the Ubuntu
    session. To get GNOME's own behaviour back for a key, set it in /etc/dconf/db/local.d (read above the Vitre defaults) or in your own
    settings.
  * No Yaru layer under the shell theme (see above).
  * vitre-system carries the boot splash only. Neither distribution has a login screen theme switch that a package can use.

If you used Vitre's per-user install before
  The per-user install (install.sh) puts copies of the extensions in ~/.local/share/gnome-shell/extensions, the theme in ~/.themes and
  the settings in your own dconf database. In the Vitre session your copies win over the packaged ones (the same uuid is found in your
  folder first), so you would keep running the old files after an upgrade. Before the first login to the Vitre session, run
  uninstall.sh of the per-user package: it removes those copies and puts the settings it changed back. The packaged Vitre session
  does not need the per-user install at all.
